    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\n**EventEmitter** should be explained from its actual runtime or design contract, then demonstrated with a small TypeScript example.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nState the rule in one sentence, trace a small example from input to output, and identify the important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype Handler<T> = (value: T) => void;\n\nclass EventEmitter<T> {\n  private listeners: {\n    event: string;\n    handler: Handler<T>;\n  }[] = [];\n\n  on(event: string, handler: Handler<T>): void {\n    this.listeners.push({ event, handler });\n  }\n\n  emit(event: string, value: T): void {\n    for (let i = 0; i < this.listeners.length; i += 1) {\n      if (this.listeners[i].event === event) {\n        this.listeners[i].handler(value);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype Handler<T> = (value: T) => void;\n\nclass EventEmitter<T> {\n  private listeners =\n    new Map<string, Set<Handler<T>>>();\n\n  on(event: string, handler: Handler<T>): void {\n    const set =\n      this.listeners.get(event) ??\n      new Set<Handler<T>>();\n\n    set.add(handler);\n    this.listeners.set(event, set);\n  }\n\n  emit(event: string, value: T): void {\n    this.listeners.get(event)\n      ?.forEach((handler) => handler(value));\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **EventEmitter** only where it solves a real production requirement; define ownership, cleanup, failure handling, and observability explicitly.\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck empty/nullish inputs, repeated calls, boundaries, failure, cleanup, memory retention, concurrency, and browser/Node differences where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the semantic rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and why the production approach is appropriate.",
