    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\nPromise.all fulfills with ordered values when all inputs fulfill and rejects on the first rejection.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nState the rule in one sentence, trace a small example from input to output, and identify the important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nfunction promiseAll<T>(\n  tasks: readonly Promise<T>[],\n): Promise<T[]> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const result: T[] = [];\n    let completed = 0;\n\n    if (tasks.length === 0) {\n      resolve([]);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < tasks.length; i += 1) {\n      tasks[i].then(\n        (value) => {\n          result[i] = value;\n          completed += 1;\n          if (completed === tasks.length) resolve(result);\n        },\n        reject,\n      );\n    }\n  });\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst result = Promise.all([\n  Promise.resolve(1),\n  Promise.resolve(2),\n]);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **Promise.all** only where it solves a real production requirement; define ownership, cleanup, failure handling, and observability explicitly.\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck empty/nullish inputs, repeated calls, boundaries, failure, cleanup, memory retention, concurrency, and browser/Node differences where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the semantic rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and why the production approach is appropriate.",
