    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\n**LRU Cache** should be explained from its actual runtime or design contract, then demonstrated with a small TypeScript example.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nState the rule in one sentence, trace a small example from input to output, and identify the important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nclass LruCache<K, V> {\n  private entries: { key: K; value: V }[] = [];\n\n  constructor(\n    private readonly limit: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  get(key: K): V | undefined {\n    for (let i = 0; i < this.entries.length; i += 1) {\n      if (this.entries[i].key === key) {\n        const entry = this.entries[i];\n        this.entries.splice(i, 1);\n        this.entries.unshift(entry);\n        return entry.value;\n      }\n    }\n    return undefined;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\n```ts\nclass LruCache<K, V> {\n  private cache = new Map<K, V>();\n\n  constructor(\n    private readonly limit: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  get(key: K): V | undefined {\n    const value = this.cache.get(key);\n    if (value === undefined) return undefined;\n\n    this.cache.delete(key);\n    this.cache.set(key, value);\n    return value;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **LRU Cache** only where it solves a real production requirement; define ownership, cleanup, failure handling, and observability explicitly.\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck empty/nullish inputs, repeated calls, boundaries, failure, cleanup, memory retention, concurrency, and browser/Node differences where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the semantic rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and why the production approach is appropriate.",
