    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\nArray.flat recursively flattens nested arrays to the requested depth.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nState the rule in one sentence, trace a small example from input to output, and identify the important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nfunction flatPolyfill(\n  items: readonly unknown[],\n  depth: number,\n): unknown[] {\n  const result: unknown[] = [];\n\n  function visit(\n    values: readonly unknown[],\n    remaining: number,\n  ): void {\n    for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i += 1) {\n      const item = values[i];\n\n      if (Array.isArray(item) && remaining > 0) {\n        visit(item, remaining - 1);\n      } else {\n        result.push(item);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  visit(items, depth);\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst result = [1, [2, [3]]].flat(2);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **Array.flat** only where it solves a real production requirement; define ownership, cleanup, failure handling, and observability explicitly.\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck empty/nullish inputs, repeated calls, boundaries, failure, cleanup, memory retention, concurrency, and browser/Node differences where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the semantic rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and why the production approach is appropriate.",
