    difficulty: "Hard",
    experienceLevel: "5–8 Years",
    category: "JavaScript Coding Questions",
    expectedAnswer: "Retries should target transient failures, use bounded backoff/jitter, and stop at a clear limit.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\nRetries should target transient failures, use bounded backoff/jitter, and stop at a clear limit.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nState the rule in one sentence, trace a small example from input to output, and identify the important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nasync function retry<T>(\n  task: () => Promise<T>,\n  attempts: number,\n): Promise<T> {\n  let lastError: unknown;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i += 1) {\n    try {\n      return await task();\n    } catch (error) {\n      lastError = error;\n    }\n  }\n\n  throw lastError;\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\n```ts\nasync function retry<T>(\n  task: () => Promise<T>,\n  attempts: number,\n): Promise<T> {\n  for (let i = 1; i <= attempts; i += 1) {\n    try {\n      return await task();\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (i === attempts) throw error;\n    }\n  }\n\n  throw new Error(\"Unreachable\");\n}\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **Retry Function** only where it solves a real production requirement; define ownership, cleanup, failure handling, and observability explicitly.\n\nStep 6 — Edge cases:\nCheck empty/nullish inputs, repeated calls, boundaries, failure, cleanup, memory retention, concurrency, and browser/Node differences where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the semantic rule first, then implementation, complexity, failure mode, and why the production approach is appropriate.",
